  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n\nWe are to design a step function sequence minimizing the evaluator's objective: 2*n*max(conv(a,a)) / (sum(a))^2.\nThe previous program achieved 1.512105, but the evaluated step function 1.5027 requires improvement.\n\nHere's the improved approach:\n1. Start with an initial sequence (either provided parent or random) and evaluate it.\n2. Perform local optimization (subgradient + linear programming) to improve the score quickly.\n3. Then use a genetic algorithm to explore the search space for longer sequences (since sequences with 1500 symbols tend to have better attack surface).\n4. To prevent being trapped in local minima, we explore random walks through the coefficient space and use crossover operators based on pulse-shaped Gaussian sequences.\n5. The genetic algorithm works on variable-length candidates (30 to 1500 elements) and uses tournaments to avoid relying solely on the best solution.\n\nMathematicals specification:\nWe represent a sequence as a vector a of coefficients with length n (to be determined). The objective is:\n    obj = 2 * n * max_{k} (conv(a, a)[k]) / (sum_{i} a[i])**2\n\nWe scale the sequence to reach a sum >= 0.01 (handled by evaluate_sequence). Non-negative elements.\nOur optimization strategy will:\n- Use a population-based genetic algorithm (GA) that evolves solutions.\n- Each candidate sequence is represented by floating-point numbers within 0 to 1000.\n- We design crossover operations that preserve the structure of sequences without introducing elements outside the range.\n- Mutation involves adding noise and ensures non-negativity.\n- The GA must handle variable-length sequences by:\n\n6. We start the GA from a candidate (either the parent's output, the optimized sequence, or a random one) and include a resampling mechanism when fixed lengths don't help.\n\nImplementation procedure:\nWe will implement a function `run_evo_opt` using the following steps:\n   a. Initialize the GA population (all sequences of length n) with n from 30 to 1500.\n   b. For each generation, evaluate all candidates using `evaluate_sequence`.\n   c. Select parents via tournament selection (based on objective score), with the constraint of including multiple segments.\n   d. Perform crossover:\n        - Spatial crossover: combine parts of two parent sequences by swapping segments around a point.\n        - Comb-shaped crossover: divide the sequence length proportionally and cross over individual positions.\n   e. Apply mutations with Gaussian noise, respect range, and normalize after.\n   f. Purge duplicates and stagnation, then introduce diversity perturbations.\n   g. After optimization, verify the candidate and record the objective.\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n   - Use: \n        * Population size: 50 dynamically adjusted based on fitness improvements\n        * Tournament selection (random or by segments)\n        * Mutation rate: 0.1, decreasing over time\n        * Crossover rate: 0.3 to 0.8 (spatial crossover is preferred)\n        * Resampling when no improvement in 20 generations\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\nEach generation's evaluation cost: O(n log n) due to the FFT-based convolution. \nThe time budget is 1500 seconds. We implement iterative resource allocation:\n   - Start with short sequences (n=30-100) to bootstrap.\n   - Progressively try longer sequences (n=100-1500) as improvement stalls.\n   - Use a polning mechanism after each candidate's evaluation (even during the last evolution) to track progress.\n\nValidation and return contract:\nWe run `evaluate_sequence` on the final candidate and ensure:\n   - Non-negative numbers (via clipping)\n   - Sum >= 0.01 (via defensive normalization)\n   - Objective score is returned and compared\n\nFallback procedure (returning early or after failure):\nIf the genetic algorithm's best candidate is not found within the budget, return the parent sequence (or local optimization result). This fallback requires clonning and evaluating.\n\nFinally, we implement the GA step-by-step, explicitly handle length variations, and verify the candidate's score regularly.\n\nSteps in `run_evo_opt`:",
